class ConnectionRequestHandler {
private:
    std::queue<std::pair<std::string, std::string>> requestQueue;
    std::mutex queueMutex;
    std::condition_variable cv;
    DistributedDatabase db;
    bool running;

public:
    ConnectionRequestHandler() : running(false) {}

    void start() {
        running = true;
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) { // Start multiple worker threads
            std::thread(&ConnectionRequestHandler::worker, this).detach();
        }
    }

    void stop() {
        running = false;
        cv.notify_all();
    }

    void enqueueRequest(const std::string& userId, const std::string& token) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(queueMutex);
        requestQueue.push({userId, token});
        cv.notify_one();
    }

private:
    void worker() {
        while (true) {
            std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(queueMutex);
            cv.wait(lock, [this] { return !requestQueue.empty() || !running; });
            if (!running && requestQueue.empty()) {
                break;
            }
            auto [userId, token] = requestQueue.front();
            requestQueue.pop();
            lock.unlock();

            if (authenticateUser(userId, token)) {
                db.storeConnection(userId);
            } else {
                // Handle authentication failure
            }
        }
    }
};

int main() {
    ConnectionRequestHandler handler;
    handler.start();

    // Simulate incoming connection requests
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; ++i) {
        std::string userId = "user" + std::to_string(i);
        std::string token = "token" + std::to_string(i);
        handler.enqueueRequest(userId, token);
    }

    // Wait for a while to process requests
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(10));
    handler.stop();

    return 0;
}
```

This code provides a basic structure for the connection request handler component. It includes a mock user authentication API and a distributed database for storing connection metadata. The `ConnectionRequestHandler` class manages incoming requests, enqueues them, and processes them in worker threads. The `authenticateUser` function simulates the authentication process, and the `storeConnection` method stores connection metadata in the database. The main function demonstrates how to start the handler, enqueue some requests, wait for a while, and stop the handler.

This code does not include advanced features such as fallback mechanisms, distributed caching, or adaptive resource allocation. It also lacks logging and monitoring capabilities. To implement these features, you would need to extend the code with additional classes and logic, following best practices for each feature.']

